# Approach Comparison

| Metric / Aspect | Approach 1: Two Pointers | Approach 2: Single Pass DP | Approach 3: Brute Force |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Time Complexity** | $\mathcal{O}(N)$ | $\mathcal{O}(N)$ | $\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ |
| **Space Complexity** | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ |
| **Intuition Level** | High (Direct window expansion) | High (State machine transition) | Basic (Check all subarrays) |
| **Code Length** | Moderate | Very Concise | Concise |
| **LeetCode Verdict** | **Accepted** ($\sim 100\%$ speed) | **Accepted** ($\sim 100\%$ speed) | Time Limit Exceeded / Slow |

---

# Corner & Edge Cases Handled
1. **No element $\le threshold$ or no even element:** Output is correctly `0`.
2. **Single Element Array:** E.g., `nums = [2], threshold = 5` returns `1`; `nums = [3], threshold = 5` returns `0`.
3. **Repeated Even/Odd Patterns:** Handled cleanly by resetting or jumping pointers without infinite loops.
4. **Large Threshold Values:** Threshold condition passes easily without overflow issues.
